-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BASIC OPERATION
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The shell can be operated in two modes:

Interactive Mode: Start the shell by typing './myshell'. You will be presented with a prompt showing your current directory. Type commands and press 'Enter' to execute.

Batch Mode: Run the shell with a file argument: './myshell batchfile'. The shell will execute all commands within that file in order and then exit.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL COMMANDS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following commands are built directly into the shell:

cd <directory>: Changes the current working directory.

If no directory is specified, it prints the current working directory path.

It updates the 'PWD' environment variable automatically.

clr: Clears the terminal screen.

dir <directory>: Lists the contents of the specified directory. If no directory is provided, it lists the contents of the current directory.

environ: Displays all current environment variables.

echo <comment>: Prints your comment to the screen followed by a newline. Extra spaces between words are reduced to a single space.

help: Displays this user manual (readme).

pause: Suspends shell operation until the 'Enter' key is pressed.

quit: Exits the shell.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRAM INVOCATION (EXTERNAL COMMANDS)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Any command entered that is not an internal command listed above is treated as an external program. The shell will attempt to find and execute this program by creating a new "child" process.

THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT

The "environment" is a collection of variables that define the context in which programs run.

shell: This variable contains the full path to your 'myshell' executable.

parent: When you run an external program, the shell passes an environment variable called 'parent' to that program, which also points to the 'myshell' path.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
I/O REDIRECTION
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

By default, commands read input from the keyboard and send output to the screen. You can change this using redirection operators:

Output Redirection (>): Overwrites a file with the command's output.
Example: 'dir > myfiles.txt' (Creates or clears 'myfiles.txt' and saves the directory listing there).

Append Output (>>): Adds the command's output to the end of an existing file.
Example: 'echo Hello >> greetings.txt' (Adds "Hello" to the end of the file).

Input Redirection (<): Forces a program to read its data from a file instead of the keyboard.
Example: 'myprogram < input_data.txt'.

Redirection can be combined: 'program arg1 < input.txt > output.txt'.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BACKGROUND EXECUTION (&)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Normally, the shell waits for a program to finish before asking for the next command. To run a program in the "background" and keep using the shell immediately:

Add an ampersand (&) to the end of your command.

Example: 'long_calculation_program &'
The shell will launch the program and return to the prompt instantly.

End of Manual
